Now I can see the picture clearly:

- Throw side: 78 tracklets start within 108px of a hand, and 52 of 78 (66.7%) have slope > 1.0. My criteria is actually appropriate.
- Catch side: 85 tracklets end within 108px, only 38 of 85 (44.7%) have slope < -1.0. My catch criteria is too strict!

The issue: many catches involve balls held by the hand where distance isn't actually decreasing at the end (the ball is already AT the hand and disappearing). For catches, we should also accept "ball already at hand and tracklet terminates" — i.e., end_dist < some smaller threshold (ball is essentially in contact).

Let me also examine: for the trick video, we have only 76 tracklets but 33 entries. That's because the same hand gets many entries from different tracklets. This is the correct behavior at a juggling scene, BUT we need the tokens to be properly consumed. Let me see why so few exits are firing.
