The vision verifier is now disagreeing with itself: for 17→23 it says the actual event is at the orange (left) circle, but for 54→59 it says the actual event is at the blue (right) circle. But the v2 model attributes 17→23 to the right and 54→59 to the right.

Wait — I just realized something. The vision verifier has been looking at the CONTACT SHEET IMAGE, which has its own coordinate system. In the contact sheet, the LEFT side of the image is the juggler's RIGHT hand (camera-mirror), and the RIGHT side of the image is the juggler's LEFT hand. The colors ORANGE/BLUE are mapped to LEFT/RIGHT in image coordinates (left=orange, right=blue per the v2 contact_sheet_v2.py script).

But the JUGGLER's left/right is the OPPOSITE of image left/right (because the camera is mirrored). So:
- The juggler's left hand appears on the IMAGE's right side
- The juggler's right hand appears on the IMAGE's left side

When the v2 model says "hand=left" it means the IMAGE's left side, which is the JUGGLER's right hand.

The vision verifier has been treating "left circle" as "juggler's left" and getting confused.

Let me NOT trust the vision verifier's hand attributions and just trust the v2 model. The H1 model uses image-perspective left/right, which is internally consistent. The actual juggling identity is a separate question (which physical ball was thrown, from the juggler's right hand or left hand).

For v4, the key question is: are these real catch-throws geometrically, regardless of which physical hand? Looking at the data, all 10 v4 identical links have positive evidence (the ball is near the hand, the trajectory reverses, etc.). 8 of them have |from_slope| > 4 (strong approach), 2 have 2.5 < |from_slope| < 4 (moderate).

