This is a "stuck" detection on something up high. Looking at the data, at f=248-253, there's a detection at (611-618, 205-207) with conf 0.42-0.76. That's a stationary object up high — could be a sign, a tree branch, or a feature on the wall. Not a face, not a hand.

So the H4 face-mask didn't move the cluster. The cluster is at a position that's:
- 50-80px to the right of the wrist (in the hand-reach radius, so passes the H3 reach test)
- ~200px above the wrist (not below the wrist, so the "above wrist = face" mask didn't catch it)

The face-mask assumption was wrong. The detector latches onto a stationary high-up feature, NOT a face feature.

The H4 face-mask experiment is a useful negative result: simple geometric masks don't work because detector confusion can lock onto any stationary feature, not just faces. A real fix would require either a more sophisticated detector or a model that distinguishes "ball-like" features from "non-ball" features.

This is a good place to stop. The H3 + H4 work is solid. Let me write the H4 report briefly, commit, and update state.
